title: Forests through mirrors
pid: 5c52a974-1f40-4955-94f9-712304c41a39
body: |2-
  A forest is what a folder request draws (the =C-c l= commands;
  skg-for-ai's 'forest KIND ID'): the partners of a note in one
  relation, each with its containerward chain. Wherever a forest walks
  containerward it passes through mirrors: the member, and each note of
  its chain, that has a fork partner holds its forks folder, which lists
  its mirrors, each with its own containers, which pass through mirrors
  in turn ('docs/glossary.org', "foldering, the containerward chain,
  chain node"). So a containers forest says where a note sits in anyone's
  version of it:

  #+begin_example
    * N
    ** These also contain it.
    *** C              (C contains N; C forks T)
    **** (forks folder)
    ***** T            (theirs)
    ****** These also contain it.
    ******* TT         (TT contains T)
    **** CC            (CC contains C)
  #+end_example

  The chain stays raw: each note is itself, never a mirror shown in its
  place, and a set of mirrors is listed once per view, at the first of
  its notes the refresh reaches, so a cycle of containment and forks
  ends. The skgrepo restriction gates the forks folder as any: a mirror
  it keeps out is not listed, or, where only its home is kept out and
  the record shows, is a restricted headline.

  The other traversals:
  - A content view's contentward tree passes through mirrors by
    substitution ("Mirror substitution"), and an editable note's forks
    folder lists the rest.
  - The step of a partner folder itself names the note's own partners,
    not its mirrors': a link names one ID, and a hide one note in one
    forkee's content. Its members' chains pass through mirrors as above.
  - The forks folder lists every mirror already, flat.
  - The diff view draws no forks folder in a chain, as it substitutes
    nothing: its chains place a change, and a mirror of an unchanged
    container would not help.
  - Org export follows the stored =contains=.
